Now we first started this video tutorial series

5
00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:13.199
talking about object oriented concepts.

6
00:00:13.199 --> 00:00:16.539
Remember this deals with the parent child relationship

7
00:00:16.539 --> 00:00:19.559
where parents establish default functionality

8
00:00:19.559 --> 00:00:21.320
that's then passed down to the children

9
00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:23.879
and the children can override or extend

10
00:00:23.879 --> 00:00:25.719
that default functionality

11
00:00:25.719 --> 00:00:28.399
that they receive from the parents.

12
00:00:28.399 --> 00:00:29.679
We then took a look at the difference

13
00:00:29.679 --> 00:00:32.119
between the controller and pawn classes

14
00:00:32.119 --> 00:00:34.719
and why we wanna have one single controller

15
00:00:34.719 --> 00:00:36.719
control multiple pawns.

16
00:00:36.719 --> 00:00:40.320
We then moved on to working with blueprint interfaces

17
00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:44.820
and why these are important, not only for optimization,

18
00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:48.179
but also makes it very easy

19
00:00:48.179 --> 00:00:50.979
to have blueprints interact with one another.

20
00:00:50.979 --> 00:00:54.979
We then took all of this information that we discussed

21
00:00:54.979 --> 00:00:57.899
and put it to use by creating a light switch

22
00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:01.659
that when activated can turn off a specific light

23
00:01:01.659 --> 00:01:04.760
and we follow that up with extending that light switch

24
00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:07.079
to allow us to select multiple lights

25
00:01:07.079 --> 00:01:09.980
so the same light switch would turn multiple lights off

26
00:01:09.980 --> 00:01:11.719
instead of just a single one.

27
00:01:11.719 --> 00:01:15.920
I hope you enjoyed this video tutorial series.

28
